  <meta name="description" content="One side of the accommodation conversation has been automated. The other side is a person having a hard week. That asymmetry is new, and it is why this exists now."/>
  <title>Why now | Curb Cut</title>
</head>
<body>
<c:CurbCutShell pageTitle="Why now" current="why"
  eyebrow="Why this, and why now"
  heading="One side of this conversation got an agent. The other side got a form."
  lede="That asymmetry is new. It's the whole reason for building this in 2026 rather than admiring the problem for another decade.">

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-the-thing-that-works">
    <p class="sec-label">The finding everything rests on</p>
    <h2 id="sec-the-thing-that-works">The thing that works is the thing almost nobody does</h2>
    <p>
      Across 655 large employers in 2025, the median share of staff who told
      their employer about a disability was <strong>3.5%</strong>, and it did
      not rise that year. Among college-educated people in white-collar jobs in
      the United States, an older study found <strong>30% have a disability</strong>
      under the federal definition and <strong>3.2% tell their employer</strong>.
      Two studies, a decade apart, one number.
    </p>
    <p>
      It isn't a white-collar problem. In 2025, people with a disability in the
      United States were twice as likely to work part time and more often in
      service, production and transport jobs. By 2026, 98% of large employers
      report accommodation programmes. The programmes exist. The asking doesn't.
    </p>
    <p>
      That gap isn't caused by the outcome being bad. Of the people who did
      disclose, <strong>83% said it got them better support</strong>. Disclosure
      works. It works for five out of six people who try it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this isn't a population making a cost-benefit calculation and landing
      on no. Something is stopping people at the door, before the calculation.
      What is stopping them is the asking itself: finding the policy, learning
      its words, holding a work login, filling in the form, and saying a
      sentence about your own body to somebody who will still be your manager
      on Monday.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>And the answer, when people do ask, is usually yes and usually
        free.</strong> Of the employers who gave the Job Accommodation Network
        cost figures, 61% said the accommodation cost them nothing. A third
        reported a one-time cost with a median of $300. Two thirds called it
        very or extremely effective. Twelve per cent said it didn't work, which
        is worth stating, because a page that only quotes the two thirds is
        selling something.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      A cheap, effective, mostly-granted thing that almost nobody requests. The
      bottleneck was never money and it was never willingness. It has always
      been the request.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-meanwhile-the-employer-s">
    <p class="sec-label">What changed</p>
    <h2 id="sec-meanwhile-the-employer-s">Meanwhile, the employer's side of it was quietly automated</h2>
    <p>
      At an EEOC public hearing in January 2023, the estimate put on the record
      was that <strong>as many as 83% of employers, and up to 99% of the
      Fortune 500, use some automated tool to screen or rank candidates</strong>.
      Treat that as an estimate rather than a measurement &mdash; it's quoted
      as one. It's still enough to say that at a large employer, something
      automated reads you before a person does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The EEOC's own guidance names three ways those systems fail disabled
      people in particular. None of them require anybody to intend harm.
    </p>
    <ul class="negs">
      <li>
        <b>They screen you out for a disability-related trait.</b> An employment
        gap that was treatment. Speech a voice model scores as low confidence.
        Input speed on a timed assessment.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>They ask a medical question without anyone deciding to ask one.</b>
        A pre-offer disability-related inquiry is prohibited under the ADA. A
        tool can conduct one as a side effect of what it measures, and often
        nobody in the building knows it happened.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>They give you nowhere to ask for an adjustment to the process
        itself.</b> A timed assessment with no way to request more time is a
        closed door with no handle on your side.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-every-rule-so-far">
    <p class="thesis">
      So the disabled person now negotiates with a system, <em>using a PDF.</em>
    </p>
    <p class="thesis-sub">
      This is the part that's genuinely new. For most of the history of this
      problem, both sides of an accommodation conversation were people, moving
      at the speed of people. Now one side runs at machine scale, applies the
      same rule to fifty thousand applicants before lunch, and never gets tired.
      The other side is one person, on a hard day, being asked to explain
      themselves in a box.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-every-rule-so-far-2">
    <p class="sec-label">Why regulation isn't the whole answer</p>
    <h2 id="sec-every-rule-so-far-2">id="sec-every-rule-so-far">Every rule so far governs their machine. None of them gives you one.</h2>
    <p>
      California's rules on automated decision systems in employment took effect
      on 1 October 2025. The EEOC has kept algorithmic bias in its sights
      through 2026, with an expectation that employers audit these tools.
      This is real progress and it should continue.
    </p>
    <p>
      But look at what all of it does. It constrains the system that's pointed
      <em>at</em> you. It makes the screening fairer, the audit more likely, the
      liability clearer. What none of it does is hand you anything. After every
      one of those rules lands, the person who can't type for long still has to
      find the policy, learn its words, and say the sentence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>There's no reason the capability should only run in one
      direction.</strong> The same technology that reads fifty thousand
      applicants can sit on the other side of the table and know the answer to
      "what am I even allowed to ask for" &mdash; which is the question almost
      nobody can answer, and the reason almost nobody asks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-an-agent-pointed-the">
    <p class="sec-label">What this is</p>
    <h2 id="sec-an-agent-pointed-the">An agent pointed the other way</h2>
    <p>
      Curb Cut belongs to the person, not to the employer. It knows a sourced
      library of adjustments and what they typically cost. It will write the
      request in your words. It won't send it until you say yes, and a hedge
      isn't a yes.
    </p>
    <p>
      And it never asks what you've. Not as a courtesy &mdash; there's no
      field for a diagnosis anywhere in the system, so a manager who asks is
      told that nobody knows and nobody can find out. The 3.2% number at the top
      of this page is a measurement of how much people fear that question. The
      correct response to it isn't to promise discretion. It's to make the
      question unanswerable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-what-an-agent-built">
    <p class="sec-label">The uncomfortable part</p>
    <h2 id="sec-what-an-agent-built">What an agent built for disabled people could do to them</h2>
    <p>
      Pointing a capable agent at this problem creates new ways to hurt the
      people it's for. These aren't hypotheticals we're being careful about.
      Each one is a specific harm with a specific thing built to prevent it, and
      each is enforced somewhere a reader can check.
    </p>
    <table class="facts">
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>It becomes a new place to disclose</th>
          <td>An assistant that's easy to talk to collects more than a form
              does. So there's no field for a diagnosis, condition, disability
              type, medical note, severity or prognosis. Not restricted &mdash;
              absent. An automated check fails the build if anyone adds one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>It guesses, confidently</th>
          <td>A fluent wrong answer to "what could I ask for" sends someone to
              their manager with a bad request and costs them the ask. Options
              come from a sourced library or not at all; when there's no match
              it says so rather than improvising.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>It acts on an ambiguous yes</th>
          <td>"I guess so" is how people agree to things they don't want. The
              code that creates a request refuses to run without explicit
              approval, and a test proves the refusal.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>It becomes another thing you can't escape</th>
          <td>Being stuck with a machine is the specific failure mode disabled
              people already know from phone trees. A person is always
              reachable, and always reaches you by message, never by telephone.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>It profiles the people it serves</th>
          <td>Inferred disability status is a category that should not exist.
              Nothing here is scored, predicted or inferred. Every signal is one
              the person set, in their own words.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>It makes you audition for the bot</th>
          <td>Explaining yourself to a machine to earn a human is a worse
              version of the original problem. You can ask for a person at any
              point, including as your first message, without saying why.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-it-is-thursday">
    <p class="sec-label">Who this is actually for</p>
    <h2 id="sec-it-is-thursday">It's Thursday</h2>
    <p>
      You haven't slept properly since Sunday, because the new seating plan put
      you under a light that hums. You already know what would fix it. You have
      known for three weeks.
    </p>
    <p>
      What you don't know is how to say it. Not the words &mdash; the
      consequence. Whether it goes in a file. Whether it comes up in March.
      Whether the person you tell is the person who decides about the promotion,
      and whether they will remember you as someone who needed something.
    </p>
    <p>
      So you do the arithmetic everybody in this position does, and you decide
      you'll manage, because managing is a known quantity and asking isn't.
      Then the form asks you to describe your limitations in the box provided.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Curb Cut is built for that Thursday.</strong> Not for the policy,
      not for the audit, and not for the employer's compliance position. For the
      forty seconds in which somebody decides it isn't worth it, and is right,
      because the way we've built this has made them right.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-where-the-numbers-come">
    <h2 id="sec-where-the-numbers-come">Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p>
      Every figure on this page is recorded with its source, its date and its
      denominator, including the ones that weaken the argument. That file is in
      the repository as <span class="kw">docs/EVIDENCE.md</span>, and it exists
      because every claim on this page links to the code or the run that backs it.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/ask">Try it <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
      <a class="btn" href="/">What it does</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</c:CurbCutShell>
